Genealogy of Jesus Christ
The book of the birth of Jesus Christ, son of David, son of Abraham:
Ibrahim is Isaac's son. And Isaac is Jacob's son. And Jacob is Judah's son and his brothers'.
Judah fathered Perez and Zerah by Tamar. And Perez fathered Hezron. And Hezron fathered Ram.
Aram was the son of Amminadab; Amminadab was the son of Nahshon; Nahshon was the son of Salmon.
Salmon was the son of Boaz by Rahab. And Boaz was the son of Obed by Ruth. And Obed was the son of Jesse.
Wisai is David's son. And King David was Solomon's son from Uriah's wife.
Solomon was Rehoboam's son. And Rehoboam was Abijah's father. And Abijah was Asa's father.
Asa was the son of Jehoshaphat, who was the son of Jehoram, who was the son of Uzziah.
Uzziah was the son of Jotham; Jotham was the son of Ahaz; Ahaz was the son of Hezekiah.
Hezekiah was the son of Manasseh. Manasseh was the son of Amon. And Amon was the son of Josiah.
Josiah was the son of Jehoiachin and his brothers during the Babylonian exile.
After Babylon was taken captive, Jeconiah had Shealtiel. And Shealtiel had Zerubbabel.
Zerubbabel was the son of Shealtiel, who was the son of Neri, who was the son of Malchi.
And Azor was the son of Zadok; and Zadok was the son of Ahimaaz; and Ahimaaz was the son of Eliud.
And Eliud was the father of Eleazar, and Eleazar the father of Matthan, and Matthan the father of Jacob.
Jacob was the father of Joseph, Mary's husband, who gave birth to Jesus, who is called Christ.
All generations from Abraham to David were fourteen generations; and from David until the exile to Babylon, fourteen generations; and from the exile to Babylon until the Messiah, fourteen generations. The birth of Jesus Christ
This is how Jesus Christ was born: When Mary his mother was engaged to Joseph, before they came together, she became pregnant by the Holy Spirit.
Joseph's leg was broken, but he didn't want to make it public; he wanted to keep it secret.
But while he was thinking about these things, an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream and said, "Joseph son of David, do not be afraid to take Mary home as your wife, because what is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit."
She will give birth to a son, and you are to give him the name Jesus, because he will save his people from their sins."
And all this was done so that what had been said by the Lord through his prophet would be fulfilled:
Behold, the virgin shall conceive and bear a son, and they shall call his name Immanuel (which means God with us).
When Joseph woke up from his sleep, he did what the angel of the Lord had commanded him to do; he took his wife.
She didn't know him until she gave birth to her firstborn son. His name was called Jesus.
The Visit of the Magi
When Jesus was born in Bethlehem in Judea, during King Herod's reign, some magi from the east came to Jerusalem
They said to him, "Where is he who has been born king of the Jews? For we saw his star when it rose and have come to worship him."
When King Herod heard this, he was disturbed, and all Jerusalem with him.
He gathered all the chief priests and scribes together and asked them where Christ was to be born.
They said to him, "In Bethlehem in Judea. For this is what has been written by the prophet:"
And you, Bethlehem in the land of Judah, are not least among the rulers of Judah; for from you will come a ruler who will shepherd my people Israel."
Then Herod summoned the magi secretly and ascertained from them what time the star appeared.
Then he sent them to Bethlehem and said, "Go and search carefully for the child. When you find him, report to me so that I too may go and worship him."
When they heard from the king, they went; and there was the star which they had seen in the east standing over where the child was.
When they saw the star, they were overjoyed with great joy.
They came to the house where they found the child with his mother Mary. They bowed down and worshipped him. Then they opened their treasures and presented him with gifts of gold and frankincense and myrrh.
Then when they were warned in a dream not to go back to Herod, they returned to their country by another route. The flight into Egypt
After they had left, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream and said, "Get up! Take the child and his mother and escape to Egypt. Stay there until I tell you, for Herod is going to search for the child to kill him."
So he got up and took his son and mother in the evening and set off for Egypt.
And this was to fulfill what had been spoken by the Lord through the prophet, saying: "Out of Egypt I called my son."
When Herod saw that he had been mocked by the wise men, he became furious and gave orders to kill all the boys in Bethlehem and its vicinity who were two years old and under, according to the time he had learned from the wise men.
Then what was said through Jeremiah the prophet came true:
A sound was heard in Ramah - weeping and great mourning - Rachel weeping for her children and refusing to be comforted, because they are no more. Return to Nazareth
When Herod died, an angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in Egypt in a dream
Saying, "Get up, take the boy and his mother and go to the land of Israel, because those who were seeking the boy's life have died."
So he got up and took the boy and his mother and came to the land of Israel.
But when he heard that Archelaus was reigning over Judea in place of his father Herod, he was afraid to go there. And being warned in a dream, he departed for the regions of Galilee.
And he came and lived in a city called Nazareth, so that what was said through the prophets might be fulfilled: "He will be called a Nazarene."
John the Baptist paves the way
In those days John the Baptist came preaching in the wilderness of Judea.
Saying: "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven has come near."
For this is what was spoken by Isaiah the prophet: "A voice cries out: 'In the wilderness prepare the way of the Lord, make straight in the desert a highway for our God.'"
John wore clothing made of camel's hair, with a leather belt around his waist. His diet consisted of locusts and wild honey.
Then all Judea and those from Jerusalem went to him, and all the region around the Jordan.
They relied on him in Jordan, confessing their sins.
When he saw many Pharisees and Sadducees coming to his baptism, he said to them, "Brood of vipers! Who warned you to flee from the wrath to come?"
Produce fruit in keeping with repentance.
And do not think to say in yourselves: We have Abraham for our father; because I tell you that God is able from these stones to raise up children unto Abraham.
Now I have put my axe to the root of the tree; every tree that does not bear good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
I baptize you with water for repentance, but he who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to carry. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit and fire.
"He will thresh his grain with his flail, winnow it with his fan, and store his grain in his barn; but he will burn the chaff with an unquenchable fire." Baptism of Jesus Christ
Then Jesus came from Galilee to John at the Jordan to be baptized by him.
But John stopped him saying: "I need to be baptized by you, and do you come to me?"
Jesus replied, "Let it be so now; it is proper for us to fulfill all righteousness." Then he allowed him.
When Jesus was baptized, he went up from the water. At that moment heaven was opened, and he saw the Spirit of God descending like a dove and lighting on him.
And there came a voice from heaven saying, "This is my beloved Son in whom I am well pleased."
Jesus faces temptation
Then Jesus was led up by the Spirit into the wilderness to be tempted by the devil.
After fasting for forty days and nights, he finally got hungry.
The tempter came to him and said, "If you're God's son, tell these stones to turn into bread."
He answered, "It is written: Man shall not live by bread alone, but by every word that comes from the mouth of God."
Then Satan took him to the holy city and set him on the temple's wing
And he said to him, "If you are the Son of God, throw yourself down; for it is written, 'He will give his angels charge over you,' and 'On their hands they will bear you up, lest you strike your foot against a stone."
Jesus said to him, "It is also written: 'Do not put the Lord your God to the test.'"
Then Satan took him to a very high mountain and showed him all the kingdoms of the world and their splendor.
He said to him: "I'll give you all these if you prostrate yourself before me."
Then Jesus said to him, "Get behind me, Satan! For it is written: 'Worship the Lord your God, and serve Him only.'"
Then Satan left him alone, and angels came to serve him. The beginning of his ministry was in Galilee.
When Jesus heard that John had been arrested, he withdrew to Galilee.
He left Nazareth and came to live in Capernaum by the sea, in the region of Zebulun and Naphtali.
To fulfill what was spoken by Isaiah the prophet:
The land of Zebulun and the land of Naphtali, by the way of the sea, beyond Jordan, Galilee of the Gentiles.
The people who sat in darkness have seen a great light, and those who dwelt in the land of the shadow of death, upon them has light shined.
From that time Jesus began to preach, saying, "Repent, for the kingdom of heaven is at hand." The call of the first disciples
As Jesus was walking beside the Sea of Galilee, he saw two brothers, Simon called Peter and his brother Andrew. They were casting a net into the lake, for they were fishermen.
He said to them, "Come after me, and I will make you fishers of men."
He left the window and followed him.
Then he went from there and saw two other brothers, James son of Zebedee and John his brother, in their boat with Zebedee their father, mending their nets; and he called them.
They left their boat and father to follow him. Jesus was teaching and healing the sick
And Jesus went throughout all Galilee, teaching in their synagogues, preaching the gospel of the kingdom, and healing every sickness and disease among the people.
His fame spread throughout all of Syria. So they brought him all the sick people suffering from various diseases and pains, along with lunatics, epileptics, and paralyzed patients, whom he cured.
Many people from Galilee, Decapolis, Jerusalem, Judea, and across the Jordan followed him.
The Sermon on the Mount - The Beatitudes
When he saw the crowds, he went up the mountain; and when he sat down, his disciples came to him.
He opened his mouth and taught them saying:
Blessed are the poor in spirit, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are those who mourn, for they will be comforted.
Blessed are the meek, for they shall inherit the earth.
Blessed are those who hunger and thirst for righteousness, for they shall be satisfied.
Blessed are those who show mercy, for they will be shown mercy.
Blessed are the pure in heart, for they shall see God.
Blessed are the peacemakers, for they shall be called sons of God.
Blessed are those who have been excluded for righteousness' sake, for theirs is the kingdom of heaven.
Blessed are you when people insult you, persecute you and falsely say all kinds of evil against you because of me.
Rejoice and be glad, for your reward is great in heaven; for so they persecuted the prophets who were before you. You are the salt of the earth; but if the salt has lost its savor, how shall it be seasoned? It is then good for nothing but to be thrown out and trampled underfoot by men.
"You are the salt of the earth; but if the salt loses its flavor, how shall it be seasoned? It is then good for nothing but to be thrown out and trampled underfoot by men."
أنتم نور العالم. مش ممكن المدينة اللي فوق الجبل تتخفي.
They do not light a lamp and put it under a bowl, but on a stand; and it gives light to all in the house.
Let your light shine before men in such a way that they may see your good works, and glorify your Father who is in heaven. Completion of the Law
Do not think that I came to abolish the Law or the Prophets; I did not come to abolish but to fulfill.
I tell you the truth, until heaven and earth disappear, not one dot or comma will be lost from the law until everything is accomplished.
Whoever breaks one of these least commandments and teaches others to do so will be called least in the kingdom of heaven. But whoever does them and teaches them will be called great in the kingdom of heaven.
But I tell you that unless your righteousness surpasses that of the scribes and Pharisees, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven. Anger
You have heard that it was said to those of old, "Do not murder; he who murders shall be liable to judgment."
But I say to you that everyone who is angry with his brother will be liable to judgment; whoever insults his brother will be liable to the council; and whoever says, 'You fool!' will be liable to the hell of fire.
If you bring your offering to the altar and there remember that your brother has something against you, leave your offering before the altar and go; first be reconciled with your brother, and then come and offer your gift.
Leave your gift there in front of the altar; first go and be reconciled to your brother, then come and offer your gift.
Be quick to make peace with your opponent while you're still in his company, so that he doesn't hand you over to the judge, who will then hand you over to the police officer, and you end up in jail.
I have to tell you: Don't leave there until you pay off the last penny! The fornication
You have heard that it was said to those of old: You shall not commit adultery.
But I say to you that whoever looks at a woman lustfully has already committed adultery with her in his heart.
If your right eye causes you to sin, pluck it out and throw it away from you; for it is better that one of your members perish than that your whole body should be thrown into hell.
If your right hand causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away; for it is better that one of your members perish than that your whole body go to hell. Divorce
And it was said: If someone divorces his wife, he should give her a divorce document.
But I tell you that anyone who divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, makes her commit adultery, and anyone who marries a divorced woman commits adultery.
Also you have heard that it was said to those who lived long ago, "Do not break your oath, but keep the promises you make to the Lord."
But I tell you, don't swear at all; either yes or no, but anything beyond that comes from evil.
Neither by the earth because it is his footstool, nor by Jerusalem because it is the city of the great King.
And don't swear by your head, because you can't make one hair white or black.
But let your yes be yes, and your no be no; anything beyond this comes from evil. Revenge
Have you heard that it was said: "An eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth."
But I tell you, do not resist an evil person. If someone strikes you on your right cheek, turn the other also.
And if someone wants to sue you for your shirt, let them have your coat too.
If you're mocked by one mile, go with two.
Give to those who ask you for something, and don't turn away anyone who wants to borrow from you. Love your enemies.
Have you heard it said: Love your neighbor and hate your enemy?
But I tell you, love your enemies and pray for those who persecute you, that you may be children of your Father in heaven. He causes his sun to rise on the evil and the good, and sends rain on the righteous and the unrighteous.
So that you may be sons of your Father in heaven; for he makes his sun rise on the evil and on the good, and sends rain on the just and on the unjust.
For if you love those who love you, what reward have you? Do not even tax collectors do the same?
And if you greet your brothers only, what special thing have you done? Are not even tax collectors doing that?
Be perfect, therefore, as your heavenly Father is perfect.
Charity
Be careful that you don't do your good deeds in front of people to be seen by them. If you do, you will have no reward from your Father in heaven.
So when you give to charity, don't announce it with trumpets, as the hypocrites do in synagogues and on street corners, to be honored by men. I tell you the truth, they have received their reward in full.
But when you give to charity, let your left hand not know what your right is doing.
But when you pray, go away by yourself, shut the door behind you, and pray to your Father in private. Then your Father, who sees everything, will reward you. Prayer
And when you pray, do not be like the hypocrites; for they love to stand and pray in the synagogues and at the street corners, so that they may appear before men. Truly I say to you, they have their reward in full.
But when you pray, go to your private room, shut yourself in, and pray to your Father who is unseen. And your Father, who sees everything, will reward you.
And when you pray, do not use vain repetitions as the Gentiles do. For they think that they will be heard for their many words.
Don't be like them. For your Father knows what you need before you ask him.
This is how you pray: Our Father in heaven, hallowed be your name.
Your kingdom come. Your will be done, on earth as it is in heaven.
Give us today our daily bread.
Forgive us our sins, just as we also forgive those who sin against us.
And do not lead us into temptation, but deliver us from evil. For Yours is the kingdom and the power and the glory forever. Amen.
If you forgive others when they sin against you, your heavenly Father will also forgive you.
If you don't forgive people for their mistakes, your father won't forgive yours either. Fasting
And when you fast, do not look gloomy like the hypocrites, for they disfigure their faces that people may know that they are fasting. Truly I say to you, they have received their reward in full.
But you, when you're silent, just oil your head and wash your face.
But you, when you fast, put oil on your head and wash your face, so that it will not be obvious to men that you are fasting—but only to your Father, who is unseen; and your Father, who sees what is done in secret, will reward you. Treasure in heaven
Do not store up treasures for yourselves on earth, where moth and rust consume, and where thieves break in and steal.
But store up for yourselves treasures in heaven, where moths and vermin do not destroy, and where thieves do not break in and steal.
For where your treasure is, there will your heart be also.
The lamp of the body is the eye; if therefore your eye is good, your whole body will be full of light.
If your eye is evil, your whole body will be dark; if then the light that is in you is darkness, how great is that darkness!
No one can serve two masters; for either he will hate the one and love the other, or else he will hold to the one and despise the other. You cannot serve God and money. May God take care of us
That is why I tell you, do not worry about your life, what you will eat or drink; nor for your body, what you will wear. Is not life more than food, and the body more than clothing?
Look at the birds in the sky; they do not sow or reap or gather into barns, and yet your heavenly Father feeds them. Are you not more valuable than they?
Who among you can increase his stature by one cubit if he wishes?
Why do you worry about clothes? Look at how the lilies of the field grow. They don't labor or spin.
But I tell you that even Solomon in all his glory was not dressed like one of these.
If even grass in the field that is here today and tomorrow thrown into the oven God clothes like this, will he not much more clothe you, O men of little faith?
Don't worry about what you will eat or drink or wear.
For all these things the nations seek after. But your heavenly Father knows that you need all these things.
But seek first his kingdom and his righteousness, and all these things will be given to you as well.
Do not worry about tomorrow, for tomorrow will worry about itself. Each day has enough trouble of its own.
Judging others
Do not judge others so that you will not be judged.
For with whatever judgment you judge, you will be judged; and with whatever measure you measure, it will be measured to you.
Why do you look at the speck of sawdust in your brother's eye and pay no attention to the plank in your own eye?
Or how can you say to your brother, "Let me take the speck out of your eye," when all the time there's a plank in your own eye?
Hypocrite! First remove the plank from your own eye, and then you will see clearly to remove the speck from your brother's eye!
Do not give what is holy to dogs; nor cast your pearls before swine, lest they trample them under their feet, and turn and tear you in pieces. Ask, seek, knock
Ask, you will receive. Seek, you will find. Knock, it will be opened to you.
For everyone who asks receives; he who seeks finds; and to him who knocks, it will be opened.
Or if your son asks for bread, will you give him a stone?
If he asks for fish, will you give him one alive?
If you who are evil know how to give good gifts to your children, how much more will your Father in heaven give good things to those who ask him!
So whatever you want people to do for you, do also the same for them, because this is the law and the prophets. The narrow gate
Enter through the narrow gate; for wide is the gate and broad is the way that leads to destruction, and there are many who go in by it.
How narrow is the gate and how cramped the road that leads to life, and few find it! The tree and its fruit
Beware of false prophets who come to you in sheep's clothing but inwardly are ravenous wolves!
By their fruits you shall know them. Do they gather grapes from thorns or figs from thistles?
Thus every good tree bears good fruit, but a bad tree bears bad fruit.
A good tree cannot produce bad fruit, nor can a bad tree produce good fruit.
Every tree that doesn't produce good fruit is cut down and thrown into the fire.
By their fruits you shall know them.
Not everyone who says to me, 'Lord, Lord,' will enter the kingdom of heaven, but only the one who does the will of my Father who is in heaven.
Many will say to me that day: Lord, Lord! Didn't we prophesy in your name? And didn't we cast out demons in your name? And didn't we perform many miracles in your name?
Then I will declare to them plainly, "I never knew you at all! Depart from me, you evildoers!" The wise builders and the foolish builders
Anyone who listens to these words of mine and obeys them is like a wise man who built his house on rock.
Then it rained, and the rivers flooded, and the winds blew and pounded that house; yet it didn't collapse, because it had been built on solid rock.
And everyone who hears these words of mine and does not put them into practice is like a foolish man who built his house on sand.
"So it rained, and the rivers flooded, and the winds blew and struck that house, and it fell—and great was its fall!"
When Jesus had finished saying these things, the crowds were amazed at his teaching.
Because he taught them like someone who had authority, not like the scribes.
Healing leper
When he came down from the mountain, large crowds followed him.
And if an albino came to him and prostrated himself before him saying: "O my lord! If you wish, you can purify me."
Then Jesus reached out his hand and touched him, saying, "I want to; be clean!" And immediately his leprosy was cleansed.
Jesus said to him, "See that you don't tell anyone; but go, show yourself to the priest and offer for your cleansing what Moses commanded, as a testimony to them." The faith of the centurion
When Jesus entered Capernaum, a centurion came to him asking for help
He says: "Sir, my slave is lying in bed paralyzed and suffering terribly."
Jesus said to him, "I will come and heal him."
The centurion replied, "Lord, I do not deserve to have you come under my roof. But just say the word, and my servant will be healed."
"Because I too am under authority; I have soldiers under me. And I say to one, 'Go,' and he goes; and to another, 'Come,' and he comes; and to my slave, 'Do this,' and he does it."
When Jesus heard this, he was amazed and said to those who were following him, "I tell you the truth, I have never found anyone in Israel with such great faith!"
I tell you that many will come from east and west and recline at table with Abraham, Isaac, and Jacob in the kingdom of heaven, while the sons of the kingdom will be thrown into the outer darkness. There people will weep and gnash their teeth.
But those who belong to God's kingdom will be thrown outside into the darkness where there will be weeping and gnashing of teeth."
Then Jesus said to the centurion, "Go; let it be done for you according to your faith." And his slave was healed at that hour. The healing of Peter's mother-in-law and others
When Jesus came to Peter's house, he saw that his mother-in-law was lying down with a fever.
He touched her hand and she was cured; then she got up and served them.
When evening came, many demon-possessed people were brought to him. With a word he drove out the spirits, and all the sick were cured.
So that what was said through Isaiah the prophet might be fulfilled: "He took up our infirmities and carried our diseases." The price of discipleship
When Jesus saw large crowds around him, he ordered them to go across to the other side.
A writer came forward and said to him: "I will follow you wherever you go."
Jesus replied, "Foxes have dens and birds have nests, but the Son of Man has no place to lay his head."
And one of his disciples said to him, "Master, let me first go and bury my father."
Jesus said to him, "Follow me, and let the dead bury their own dead." Calming the storm
When he got into the boat his disciples followed him.
And if there was a great disturbance in the sea so that the waves covered the ship, he would be sleeping.
His disciples came to him and woke him up, saying, "Lord, save us! We're going down!"
He said to them, "Why are you so afraid? You have little faith." Then he got up and rebuked the winds and the waves, and it was completely calm.
The people were amazed and asked, "Who is this man? Even the winds and waves obey him!" - Casting Out Demons
When he came to the other side, to the region of the Gerasenes, two demon-possessed men met him, coming out of the tombs, so fierce that no one could pass that way.
And they cried out, saying, "What have you to do with us, Jesus, Son of God? Have You come here to torment us before the time?"
And far away from them was a large herd of pigs grazing.
The demons begged him, saying, "If you drive us out, send us to the herd of pigs."
He said to them, "Go." So they went off and found the herd of pigs grazing in the hills. Suddenly all the pigs rushed down the steep bank into the lake and were drowned.
But the shepherds ran away to the city and told everyone what had happened, including about the crazy men.
So all the city came out to meet Jesus; but when they saw him, they begged him to leave their territory.
Healing paralyzed
He got into the boat and crossed over to his own town.
And when they presented him to Jesus, who was sitting in front of them, he said to the paralytic, "Take courage, my child! Your sins are forgiven."
And if some people in the crowd said to themselves, "This man is blaspheming!"
But Jesus knew their thoughts, and said to them, "Why do you think evil in your hearts?"
Which is easier to say: "Your sins are forgiven" or "Get up and walk"?
But so that you may know that the Son of Man has authority on earth to forgive sins”—and he said to the paralytic, “Get up, take your mat and go home!”
He got up and went home.
When they saw this, they were all amazed and praised God for giving such authority to men. Matthew's call
As Jesus was passing by, he saw a man named Matthew sitting at the tax collector's booth. He said to him, "Follow me." So he got up and followed him.
While he was reclining at the table in his house, many tax collectors and sinners came and ate with Jesus and his disciples.
When the Pharisees saw this, they said to his disciples, "Why does your teacher eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
When Jesus heard this, he said to them, "Those who are well have no need of a physician, but those who are sick."
Go learn what this means: 'I desire mercy, not sacrifice.' For I have not come to call the righteous but sinners." The question about fasting
Then came to him the disciples of John, saying, "Why do we fast so often and your disciples don't?"
Jesus said to them, "Can you make wedding guests fast while the groom is with them? But there will come days when the bridegroom is taken away from them; then they will fast in those days."
No one sews a patch from new cloth onto an old garment; otherwise, the tear in the garment will become worse.
They don't put new wine in old wineskins, or else the skins will burst and the wine spill and the wineskins be ruined. Instead, they put new wine in fresh wineskins and thus both are preserved." The raising of Jairus' daughter and healing of the woman with bleeding
As he was speaking to them, suddenly a ruler came in and bowed down before him, saying, "My daughter has just died; but come and lay your hand on her, and she will live."
Then Jesus got up, followed by him and his disciples.
And if a woman who has had bleeding for twelve years came up behind him and touched his clothes,
Because she said to herself, "If I can just touch his clothes, I'll be healed."
Jesus turned around and saw her and said, "Daughter, your faith has made you well; go in peace." And from that moment the woman was healed.
When Jesus came to the house of the ruler, he saw the flute players and the crowd making noise.
He said to them, "Stop making such a fuss about this young girl - she is sleeping; she is not dead." But they laughed at him.
When he took her hand, she got up immediately.
The news spread throughout that whole region. Healing two blind men
As Jesus was passing by, two blind men followed him, crying out, "Have mercy on us, Son of David!"
When he came to the house, the blind men approached him, and Jesus said to them, "Do you believe that I am able to do this?" They replied, "Yes, Lord!"
Then he touched their eyes saying, "According to your faith be it unto you."
Then their eyes were opened, but they recognized him; and he disappeared from their sight. Then Jesus sternly ordered them, "Don't tell anyone!"
But they went out and spread it all over that whole region. Healing an outcast
As they were leaving, a deaf-mute madman was brought to him.
When Satan was cast out, the mute man spoke, and all were amazed saying, "Never has anything like this happened in Israel!"
But the Pharisees said, "It is by Beelzebul, the prince of demons, that he drives out demons." A few
And Jesus went throughout all the cities and villages, teaching in their synagogues and proclaiming the gospel of the kingdom and healing every disease and every affliction among the people.
When he saw the crowds, he had compassion for them, because they were harassed and helpless, like sheep without a shepherd.
Then he said to his disciples, "The harvest is plentiful but the workers are few."
"So ask the Lord who is in charge of the harvest to send some workers to his harvest field."
Sending the twelve
Then he called his twelve disciples together and gave them authority to drive out evil spirits and to heal every disease and sickness.
And here are the names of the twelve apostles: Simon who is called Peter, his brother Andrew; James son of Zebedee, and John his brother.
Phillips, Bartholomew. Thomas, Matthew the tax collector. James son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus called Lebbaeus.
Simon the Cananaean, and Judas Iscariot who betrayed him.
These twelve Jesus sent forth, and commanded them, saying, Go not into the way of the Gentiles, and into any city of the Samaritans enter ye not:
But go rather to the lost sheep of the house of Israel.
As you go, proclaim this message: "The kingdom of heaven has come near."
Heal the sick. Raise the dead. Cleanse lepers. Cast out demons. You received without payment; give without payment.
Do not acquire gold or silver or copper in your regions.
And neither bread nor clothing nor shoes nor staff shall you provide for him; for he who works is worthy of his food.
And whatever city or village you enter, find out who is worthy there, and stay with him until you leave.
And when you enter the house, greet him.
If the house is worthy, then peace be upon it; but if it's not worthy, then your peace should return to you.
And whoever does not accept you or listen to your words, leave that house or city, and shake off the dust from your feet.
I must tell you that on Judgment Day, Sodom and Gomorrah will be in a more favorable position than this city.
I am sending you out like sheep among wolves; therefore be wise as serpents and innocent as doves.
But beware of men; for they will deliver you up to councils, and in their synagogues they will scourge you.
And you will be brought before kings and rulers for my sake, as witnesses to them and to the nations.
So when you're handed over, don't worry about how or what to say, because at that moment you will be given exactly what to speak.
For you do not speak from yourselves, but the Father who sent me speaks through you.
The brother will hand over his brother to death, the father his child, children will rise up against their parents and kill them.
And you will be hated by all men for My name's sake; but he who endures to the end shall be saved.
And when they persecute you in one city, flee to another; for truly I say to you, you will not have gone through all the cities of Israel before the Son of Man comes.
The student is not better than his teacher, nor is the servant better than his master.
It's enough for the student to be like his teacher, and the servant like his master. If they called the head of household Beelzebul, how much more so those who live in his house!
So don't be afraid of them. For what is hidden will be revealed, and what is secret will become known.
Whatever you hear whispered in your ear, shout it from the rooftops!
Do not be afraid of those who kill the body but cannot kill the soul; rather, fear Him who can destroy both soul and body in hell.
Aren't two sparrows sold for a penny? Yet not one of them will fall to the ground without your Father.
But you, every hair on your head is numbered.
Don't be afraid! You are better than many birds!
Everyone who acknowledges me before men, I also will acknowledge him before my Father in heaven.
But whoever denies me in front of people, I will also deny them before my Father in heaven.
Don't think that I came to bring peace on earth; I didn't come to bring peace but a sword.
I have come to set a man against his father, a daughter against her mother, and a daughter-in-law against her mother-in-law.
A man's enemies will be those of his own household.
Whoever loves their father or mother more than me is not worthy of me; whoever loves their son or daughter more than me is not worthy of me.
And whoever does not take up his cross and follow me is not worthy of me.
Whoever finds his life will lose it, but whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
Whoever accepts you accepts me, and whoever accepts me accepts the one who sent me.
Whoever accepts a prophet by his name will receive the reward of that prophet, and whoever accepts a righteous person by their name will receive the reward of that righteous person.
And whoever gives one of these little ones just a cup of cold water to drink because he is my disciple, I tell you the truth, he will never lose his reward."
John the Baptist or Jesus?
And when Jesus had finished giving orders to his twelve disciples, he went away from there to teach and preach in their towns. Jesus and John the Baptist
But when John heard in prison about the works of Christ, he sent two of his disciples,
He said to him: "Are you next or should we wait for someone else?"
Jesus replied to them, "Go tell John what you hear and see."
The blind see, the lame walk, those with leprosy are cured, the deaf hear, the dead are raised to life, and the good news is preached to the poor.
Blessed is he who does not stumble in it.
And while these two were going, Jesus began to speak to the crowds about John: "What did you go out into the wilderness to see? A reed shaken by the wind?"
But what did you go out to see? A man dressed in soft clothing? Well, those who wear soft clothes are in kings' palaces.
But what did you go out to see? A prophet? Yes, I tell you, and more than a prophet.
For this is what was written about him: "See, I am sending an angel ahead of you to guard you along the way and bring you to the place I have prepared."
I tell you the truth, among those who were born of women there has not risen anyone greater than John the Baptist; yet he who is least in the kingdom of heaven is greater than he.
From the days of John the Baptist until now, the kingdom of heaven has been forcefully advancing, and forceful people are seizing it by force.
For all the prophets and the law prophesied until John.
And if you want to accept it, this is Elijah who was expected to come.
Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear.
And who does this generation resemble? They're like kids sitting in markets calling to their friends
They say: We played music for you but you didn't dance! We sang to you but you didn't clap!
For John came neither eating nor drinking, and they say, "He has a demon."
The Son of Man came eating and drinking, they say, "Here is a glutton and a drunkard, a friend of tax collectors and sinners." But wisdom is proved right by her deeds. Woe to you, cities where most of Jesus' miracles were performed, because you did not repent.
Then he began to rebuke the cities where most of his miracles were done because they did not repent.
Woe to you, Tyre! Woe to you, O city of Sidon! For I will extend my hand against you, declares the Lord of hosts, and I will make you a lair for jackals, a horror to all who see you. Your cities shall be desolate, your strongholds shall lie waste; and you yourselves shall become plunder and booty. You shall know that I am the Lord.
But I tell you that on the day of judgment people will be more accountable for their words than for their deeds.
And you, Capernaum, who have been exalted to heaven, will be brought down to Hades. For if the mighty works done in you had been done in Sodom, it would have remained until this day.
But I tell you that Sodom will be more bearable on the day of judgment than you.» Rest for the weary
At that time Jesus said, "I praise you, Father, Lord of heaven and earth, because you have hidden these things from the wise and learned, and revealed them to little children."
Yes, Father, because that's how it has become before you.
Everything has been given to me by my father, and no one knows the Son except the Father, nor does anyone know the Father except the Son and those to whom the Son chooses to reveal him.
Come to me all you who are weary and burdened, and I will give you rest.
Take my yoke upon you and learn from me, for I am gentle and lowly in heart, and you will find rest for your souls.
"Because Neri is cool and my load is light."
Lord of Saturday
At that time Jesus went through the grainfields on the Sabbath; his disciples were hungry and began to pick some heads of grain and eat them.
The Pharisees said to him, "Your disciples are doing what is unlawful on the Sabbath."
He said to them, "Haven't you read what David did when he was hungry along with his companions?"
How did he enter the house of God and eat the bread that was offered to him, which neither he nor those with him were allowed to eat, but only the priests?
Or haven't you read in the Law that on Sabbath days the priests in the temple desecrate what is sacred and yet are innocent?
But I tell you that there is something greater than the temple here!
If you knew what I wanted - mercy, not sacrifice - why did you condemn the innocent?
"But he is Lord even of the Sabbath."
Then he left there and came to their gathering place.
And if someone has a withered hand, they ask him, "Is it lawful to heal on the Sabbath?" so that they can accuse Him.
He said to them, "If one of you has a son or an ox that falls into a well on the Sabbath day, will you not pull him out?"
"So man is much better than sheep! That's why good deeds should be done in this spot!"
Then he said to the man, "Stretch forth your hand." So he stretched it forth, and it was restored whole like the other. The chosen one of God
When the Pharisees went out, they plotted to kill him.
Jesus taught there and then left. Large crowds followed him everywhere he went, and he healed them all.
He advised them not to show it.
To fulfill what was spoken by Isaiah the prophet:
Here is my servant whom I have chosen, my beloved in whom my soul delights; I will put my spirit upon him, and he shall proclaim justice to the nations.
He never quarrels or scolds, and one doesn't hear his voice in the streets.
A bruised reed he will not break, and a smoldering wick he will not snuff out, until he has brought justice to victory.
And his name will be called "The hope of nations." Jesus and Beelzebul
Then they brought him a blind man who was also dumb; he cured him so that the blind man could see and speak.
When they saw this, all the people said, "Could this be the son of David?"
But when the Pharisees heard this, they said, "It is only by Beelzebul, the prince of demons, that this fellow drives out demons."
But Jesus knew their thoughts, and said to them, "Every kingdom divided against itself is brought to desolation; and every city or house divided against itself will not stand."
If Satan is expelled by another devil, then he has divided against himself. How can his kingdom stand?
And if I drive out demons by Beelzebul, then by whom do your people drive them out? Therefore they will be your judges!
But if I drive out demons by Beelzebub, by whom do your people drive them out? So then they will be your judges. But if it is by the Spirit of God that I drive out demons, then the kingdom of God has come upon you.
How can anyone break into the strong man's house and steal his property unless he first ties up the strong man? Then he can plunder his house.
Whoever is not with me is against me, and whoever does not gather with me scatters.
Therefore I say to you, every sin and blasphemy will be forgiven men, but the blasphemy against the Spirit shall not be forgiven them.
And whoever speaks against the Son of Man will be forgiven; but whoever blasphemes against the Holy Spirit will never be forgiven, neither in this age nor in the age to come.
Make the tree good and its fruit good, or make the tree bad and its fruit bad; for by their fruit you will recognize them.
يا ولاد الأفعى! إزاي بتقدروا تقولوا كلام كويس وإنتو شريرين؟ عشان اللي في القلب هو اللي بيطلع على اللسان.
A good person brings forth goodness from their heart's treasure, while an evil person produces wickedness from their wicked treasure.
But I tell you that everyone will have to give account on the day of judgment for every empty word they have spoken.
"For by your words you will be justified, and by your words you will be condemned." - Jonah 1:24
Then some of the scribes and Pharisees answered him, saying, "Teacher, we want to see a sign from you."
He replied, "A wicked and adulterous generation asks for a sign, but none will be given it except the sign of Jonah."
For just as Jonah was three days and three nights in the belly of the great fish, so will the Son of Man be three days and three nights in the heart of the earth.
The men of Nineveh will stand up in judgment with this generation and condemn it; for they repented at the preaching of Jonah, and behold, something greater than Jonah is here.
The Queen of Sheba will rise in religion with this generation and guide them, because she came from the ends of the earth to hear Solomon's wisdom, and behold, one greater than Solomon is here!
If an unclean spirit comes out of a person and goes through dry places, it seeks rest but finds none.
Then he says: "I'm going back to my house from which I came." So he comes and finds it empty, swept clean, and decorated.
Then he goes away taking with him seven other spirits more wicked than himself, who go in and live there; and the last condition of that man is worse than the first. So shall it be also with this evil generation." The will of God was done.
While he was speaking to the crowd, his mother and brothers came up outside wanting to speak with him.
One man said to him, "Your mother and your brothers are standing outside, wanting to speak with you."
He replied to him saying: "Who is my mother? And who are my brothers?"
Then he stretched out his hand toward his disciples and said: "Here is my mother and my brothers."
"For whoever does the will of my Father in heaven is my brother and sister and mother."
Like the farmer and his interpretation
That day Jesus went outside to sit by the sea.
Large crowds gathered around him, so much so that he had to get into a boat and sit down while all the people were standing along the shore.
So he spoke to them in parables, saying: "The sower went out to sow."
And some fell along the path, and the birds came and ate them up.
And the last one fell among thorns; but they sprang up with it, and choked it.
But when the sun rose it burned up, since it had no roots.
And the last one fell onto the thorns, which pricked him and choked him.
And when he fell on good ground, it brought forth some hundredfold, some sixtyfold, some thirtyfold.
"Whoever has ears to hear, let them hear."
The disciples came to him and asked, "Why do you speak to them in parables?"
He replied to them, "Because it has been given you to know the secrets of the kingdom of heaven, but they have not been given."
Whoever has will be given more, but whoever doesn't have, even what they think they have will be taken away from them.
That is why I speak to them in parables; because seeing they do not see, and hearing they do not hear, nor do they understand.
This fulfills what was spoken through Isaiah the prophet: "You will be ever hearing but never understanding; you will be ever seeing but never perceiving."
For this people's heart has grown dull, and with their ears they scarcely hear, and they have closed their eyes; lest they see with their eyes and hear with their ears and understand with their hearts and turn, and I would heal them.'
But blessed are your eyes because they see, and your ears because they hear.
I tell you the truth, many prophets and righteous people longed to see what you see but didn't see it, and to hear what you hear but didn't hear it.
Listen to this parable:
Anyone who hears this message about the kingdom but doesn't understand will be taken over by the devil and end up losing whatever good seed was planted in them. This is the seed that fell beside the road.
The one who hears the word and immediately accepts it with joy is like seed planted in good soil.
But it has no root in itself; it is temporary. If there's persecution or oppression for the word, then immediately it stumbles.
The one who hears the word among thorns is like those in this world whose wealth chokes the word, making it unfruitful.
But the seed falling on good soil refers to someone with a noble and good heart, who hears God’s word and obeys it. He produces a crop—some a hundredfold, some sixty, some thirty.”
He put before them another parable, saying, "The kingdom of heaven is like a man who sowed good seed in his field."
While people were sleeping, their enemy came and planted weeds among the wheat and left.
When the plant sprouted and produced fruit, then also appeared the stork.
The servants came to their master and said to him, "Sir, didn't you plant good seed in your field? Where did these weeds come from?"
He said to them: "An enemy did this." The servants replied: "Do you want us to go get him?"
He said: No! So that you don't uproot the wheat along with the stalks when you harvest it.
Let both grow together until the harvest; and at harvest time I will say to the reapers, "First gather up the weeds and bind them in bundles to burn them up; but collect the wheat into my barn." Like a mustard seed and like yeast
He then gave them another illustration: "The kingdom of heaven is like a mustard seed that someone took and sowed in his field;
And it is the smallest of all seeds; but when it grows up, it becomes greater than all herbs, and shoots out great branches, so that the birds of the air may lodge under its shadow."
He told them another parable: "The kingdom of heaven is like yeast that a woman took and hid in three measures of flour until all of it was leavened."
This is all that Jesus spoke to the crowds in parables; without using parables he did not speak to them.
To fulfill what was said by the prophet who said: "I will open my mouth with parables, and speak things hidden since the foundation of the world." Interpretation of the parable of wheat and chaff
Then Jesus left the crowd and went into the house. His disciples came to him and said, "Explain to us the parable of the weeds in the field."
He answered them, "The sower is the Son of Man."
The field is the world. The good seed represents the children of the kingdom. And the weeds represent the children of the evil one.
The enemy who sowed it was the devil. The harvest is the end of the world. And the harvesters are angels.
As it was in the days of Noah, so will be the coming of the Son of Man.
The Son of Man will send his angels to gather from his kingdom all who cause others to stumble and those who do evil.
They will be thrown into the fiery furnace. There they will weep and gnash their teeth.
Then shall the righteous shine forth as the sun in the kingdom of their Father. He that hath ears to hear, let him hear. The parables of the treasure hid in the field, and of the pearl of great price, and of the net cast into the sea.
Also, the kingdom of heaven is like treasure hidden in a field, which someone found and hid; then because of his joy he went and sold all that he had and bought that field.
Also, the kingdom of heaven is like a merchant looking for fine pearls.
When he found one pearl that was worth a lot of money, he sold everything he owned to buy it.
It is also like a net thrown into the sea that catches fish of every kind.
When it was full they hoisted it up onto the beach, then sat down to eat the fish and fill the water-skins with wine; but all the offal from flaying them they heaved overboard.
This is how it will be at the end of the world: The angels will come out and separate the wicked from among the righteous.
And they will be cast into the furnace of fire; there shall be weeping and gnashing of teeth."
Jesus said to them, "Do you understand all this?" They answered, "Yes."
He said to them, "That is why every well-educated scribe who has been trained for the kingdom of heaven is like a homeowner who brings out of his treasure what is new and old."
When Jesus had finished these parables, he moved on from there. Nazareth rejected Jesus.
When he came to his hometown, he taught them in their synagogue. They were amazed and asked, "Where did this man get all these things? What is this wisdom that has been given him?"
Isn't this the carpenter's son? Isn't his mother named Mary, and aren't his brothers James, Joseph, Simon, and Judas?
Aren't all his sisters with us? Where did he get them from?"
They stumbled at him. But Jesus said to them, "A prophet is not without honor except in his own town and in his own home."
And many forces were not created there because they didn't believe.
Beheading John the Baptist
At that time King Herod heard about Jesus.
He said to his servants, "This is John the Baptist risen from the dead; that's why these powers work through him."
Herod had John arrested and put in prison because of Herodias, his brother Philip's wife, who was also his sister-in-law.
Because John had been telling him that it was wrong for him to have her.
When he wanted to kill him, he was afraid of the people because they considered him like a prophet.
When Herod's birthday came, his daughter danced in front of him and pleased him.
Then he swore to give her whatever she asked for.
So when she was asked by her mother, she said: "Give me here on a platter the head of John the Baptist."
The king was sad. But because of his oaths and those reclining with him, he ordered that it be given.
So he sent and had John beheaded in prison.
He brought his head to her on a plate and gave it to the girl, who took it to her mother.
His disciples came to him and lifted up the body and buried it. Then they came and told Jesus. Feeding the five thousand men
When Jesus heard this, he withdrew in a boat to a lonely place by himself. But when the crowds heard about it, they followed him on foot from many towns.
When Jesus went ashore, he saw a large crowd, had compassion on them and healed their sick.
When evening came, his disciples approached him and said, "The place is deserted and it's already late. Send the crowds away so they can go to the villages and buy themselves some food."
Jesus said to them, "They don't need to go away; you give them something to eat."
They said to him, "We have here only five loaves of bread and two fish."
He said: "Bring it to me here."
He ordered the people to sit down on the grass. Then he took the five loaves and two fish, looked up to heaven, said a blessing, broke the loaves, and gave them to his disciples, who in turn distributed them among the crowd.
They all ate until they were full, then they collected twelve baskets of leftovers.
The people who ate were about five thousand men, besides women and children. The miracle of walking on water
And when evening came, he said to his disciples that they should get into the boat and go before him to the other side, while he dismissed the crowds.
After dismissing the crowds, he went up on the mountain by himself to pray. When evening came, he was there alone.
But the ship was now in the midst of the sea, tossed by the waves; for the wind was contrary.
في الساعة الرابعة بالليل، راح يسوع ليهم ماشي على البحر.
When the disciples saw him walking on the sea, they were terrified and said, "It's a ghost!" And they cried out in fear.
But at once Jesus spoke to them, saying, "Be of good cheer! It is I; do not be afraid."
Peter answered him, "Lord, if it's you, tell me to come to you on the water."
He said, "Come." So Peter got down out of the boat, walked on the water and came toward Jesus.
But when he saw that the wind was strong, he became frightened and began to sink in the water; so he cried out, saying, "Lord, save me!"
Then Jesus reached out his hand and caught him. "O you of little faith," he said, "why did you doubt?"
When they got in the boat, the wind died down.
And those who were in the boat came up to him and fell down before him, saying, "Truly you are the Son of God!"
When they crossed over, they came to land in Gennesaret.
The people in that area knew him well. They sent for all the sick from the surrounding villages to come see him.
They asked him to touch just the fringe of his robe, and all who touched it were healed.
The pure and impure
Then some Pharisees and teachers of religious law came to Jesus from Jerusalem and asked, "Teacher, we want you to know that John the Baptist baptized with water, but why aren't you baptizing your followers with water too?"
Why do your students go beyond imitating their elders by not washing their hands when they eat bread?
And he said to them, "Why do you also transgress the commandment of God because of your tradition?"
God commands: "Honor your father and mother." And whoever curses his father or mother shall die a cursed death.
But you say to your father or mother, 'Whatever benefit I might have received from you is a gift devoted to God.' You won't honor your father or mother.
You have invalidated Allah's commandment because of your traditions!
Oh you hypocrites! Well said by Isaiah about you:
He approaches this people with his mouth, he honors me with his lips, but his heart is far from me.
And they worship Me in vain; their teachings are but human precepts."
Then he called the crowd to him and said, "Listen and understand."
"It is not what goes into your mouth that defiles you; it is what comes out of your mouth that defiles you."
Then his disciples came to him and said, "Do you know that the Pharisees were offended when they heard this?"
He answered, "Every plant that my heavenly Father has not planted will be pulled up by the roots."
Leave them alone. They're blind leaders of the blind. If one blind man leads another blind man, they will both fall into a pit."
Peter answered him, "Explain this parable to us."
Jesus said to them, "Do you still not understand?"
Don't you understand that everything we put in our mouth goes straight to the stomach and then comes out?
But what comes out of the mouth proceeds from the heart, and this defiles a person.
For from within, out of men's hearts, come evil thoughts, sexual immorality, theft, murder, adultery, greed, malice, deceit, lewdness, envy, slander, arrogance and folly. All these evils come from inside and defile a person.
"It is what comes from within that defiles a person. But when you eat without washing your hands, you are not defiled." - The faith of the Canaanite woman
Then Jesus left there and went off to the vicinity of Tyre and Sidon.
And behold, a Canaanite woman from that region came up to him, crying out, "Have mercy on me, O Lord, Son of David; my daughter is severely tormented by a demon."
But he answered her not a word. Then his disciples came to him, saying, "Send her away; for she crieth after us."
He replied, "I was sent only to the lost sheep of Israel."
She came to him and prostrated herself before him saying: "My lord, I mean!"
He answered, "It is not right to take the children's bread and toss it to their dogs."
She said: "Yes, sir! And dogs also eat from the crumbs that fall off their masters' tables!"
Then Jesus answered her, "Woman, great is your faith! Let it be done for you according to your desire." And her daughter was healed from that hour. Feeding the four thousand men
Then Jesus left there and went to the region of Galilee. He climbed up onto a mountainside and sat down there.
Large crowds came to him with people who were lame, blind, crippled, or mute, and many others. They laid them down at Jesus' feet, and he healed them.
Until all people were amazed when they saw the deaf speaking, the lame walking, the crippled walking, and the blind seeing. And they glorified God of Israel.
But Jesus called his disciples to him and said, "I have compassion for these people; they have already been with me three days and have nothing to eat. I do not want to send them away hungry, or they may collapse on the way."
His disciples said to him, "Where can we get enough bread in this deserted place to feed such a crowd?"
Jesus asked them, "How many loaves do you have?" They said, "Seven, and a few small fish."
He ordered his men to lie down on the ground.
He took the seven loaves and gave thanks and broke them, and gave them to his disciples, who distributed them to the crowd.
Everyone ate until they were full. Then they collected seven baskets of leftovers.
The people who ate were four thousand men, besides women and children.
Then he dismissed the crowds and got into the boat by himself. He went to the region of Magdala.
Ayah's request
The Pharisees and Sadducees came to test Jesus, so they asked him to show them a sign from heaven.
He replied, "When it's evening you say, 'It's going to be fair and clear because the sky is red,'
In the morning: today is winter because the sky is red with anger. You hypocrites! You know how to interpret the signs of the weather but cannot recognize the signs of the times!
A wicked generation seeks for a sign, but no sign will be given it except the sign of Jonah." Then he left them and went away. The yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees.
When his disciples came to the other side, they had forgotten to bring bread.
Jesus said to them, "Watch out! Beware of the yeast of the Pharisees and Sadducees."
They thought to themselves, "We didn't take bread."
Jesus knew what they were thinking and said to them, "Why do you think about such things in your hearts?
Don't you still understand? Don't you remember the five loaves that fed the five thousand people and how many baskets were left over?
And seven loaves for four thousand people? How many baskets did you pick up?
How is it that you don't understand? I didn't speak to you about bread. Why do you fail to perceive that I was referring to the leaven of the Pharisees and Sadducees?"
Then they understood that he was not telling them to be careful about yeast in bread but about the teaching of the Pharisees and Sadducees. Peter's confession of Christ
When Jesus came to the region of Caesarea Philippi, he asked his disciples, "Who do people say that I am?"
They said, "Some say John the Baptist; others say Elijah; still others, Jeremiah or one of the prophets."
He said to them, "But who do you say that I am?"
Simon Peter answered, "You are the Messiah, the Son of the living God!"
Jesus answered him, "Blessed are you, Simon son of Jonah! For flesh and blood has not revealed this to you, but my Father who is in heaven."
And I tell you also, that thou art Peter; and upon this rock I will build my church; and the gates of hell shall not prevail against it.
And I will give you the keys to the kingdom of heaven; whatever you bind on earth shall be bound in heaven, and whatever you loose on earth shall be loosed in heaven.”
Then he ordered his disciples to tell no one that it was Jesus of Nazareth. Jesus foretold his death and resurrection.
From that time Jesus began to show his disciples that he must go to Jerusalem and suffer many things from the elders and chief priests and scribes, and be killed, and on the third day be raised.
Peter took him aside and began to rebuke him. "God forbid, Lord!" he said. "Never mind."
But turning to Peter he said, "Get behind me, Satan! You're in my way because you don't have God's interests at heart but man's."
Then Jesus said to his disciples, "If anyone would come after me, let him deny himself and take up his cross and follow me."
Whoever wants to save his life will lose it; whoever loses his life for my sake will find it.
For what will it profit a man if he gains the whole world, and loses himself? Or what shall a man give in exchange for his soul?
The Son of Man will come in his Father's glory with his angels, and then he will reward each person according to what they have done.
I must tell you that there will be some people who won't taste death until they see the Son of Man coming in his kingdom."
The Transfiguration
After six days Jesus took with him Peter and James and John his brother, and led them up a high mountain apart by themselves.
His appearance changed in front of them; his face shone like the sun, and his clothes became white as light.
And there appeared to them Moses and Elijah, talking with him.
Peter said to Jesus, "Lord, it is good for us to be here. If you wish, we will put up three shelters—one for you, one for Moses, and one for Elijah."
And while he was speaking, a bright cloud overshadowed them, and from the cloud came a voice that said, "This is my beloved Son with whom I am well pleased; listen to him."
When the disciples heard this, they fell facedown to the ground, terrified.
Then Jesus came up to them and touched them, saying, "Get up! Don't be afraid."
They looked up and saw no one except Jesus alone.
As they were going down from the mountain, Jesus commanded them, "Don't tell anyone what you have seen until the Son of Man has risen from the dead."
And his disciples asked him, saying, "Why then say the scribes that Elias must first come?"
Jesus replied to them, "Elijah is coming first and will restore everything."
But I tell you that Elijah has already come, and they didn't recognize him; they did to him everything they wanted. And so it will be with the Son of Man also."
Then his disciples understood that he was speaking to them about John the Baptist. Healing a boy who had an evil spirit
When they came to the crowd, a man approached him kneeling down.
And he said to him, "Master, have mercy on my son, for he is an epileptic and suffers terribly; often he falls into the fire, and often into the water."
And I brought him to your disciples, but they couldn't heal him."
Jesus replied, "How long shall I put up with you, you unbelieving and perverse generation? Bring him to me."
Then Jesus rebuked him, and he came out of him. The boy was cured from that hour.
Then the disciples came to Jesus privately and said, "Why couldn't we drive it out?"
Jesus replied, "Because of your little faith. Truly I tell you, if you have faith as small as a mustard seed, you can say to this mountain, 'Move from here to there,' and it will move. Nothing will be impossible for you."
"But this type can only be removed through prayer and fasting."
While they were still talking about this, Jesus himself came up and said to them, "The Son of Man will be delivered into the hands of men."
They will kill him, but on the third day he will rise again." So they were greatly distressed. The Temple Tax
When they came to Capernaum, those who collected the two-drachma tax approached Peter and said, "Doesn't your teacher pay the temple tax?"
He said to him, "Yes." And when he was come into the house, Jesus spake unto him first, saying, What thinkest thou, Simon? of whom do the kings of the earth take custom or tribute? of their own children, or of strangers?
Peter said to him, "A foreigner." Jesus said to him, "Then if the children are free."
But so that they don't get suspicious, go to the sea and cast your net, and take the first fish you catch. When it opens its mouth, you will find a coin inside; take it and give it to them for me and yourself."
طيب، أنا هساعدك في ده! بس ممكن تقولي عايزني أترجم إيه بالظبط؟ يعني مثلاً عندك نص معين أو سؤال محدد عايز تترجمه؟